 \section{Conclusion}
 
-All three PCA strategies are profitable out-of-sample and after transaction costs, but they differ in robustness. Contrarian Treynor--Black shows that estimated alphas reverse rather than persist. It is factor neutral by construction (market beta 0.04) and performs best in crises, but it is concentrated in 2008--09, sensitive to trading costs, and has weakened since 2013. PCA pairs trading is the most robust strategy: it is profitable in 21 of 22 years, well calibrated, insensitive to its parameters, and stronger in the 2018--2023 holdout than before it. Residual snapback has the strongest signal of the three, with stocks that fall well short of the factor model's prediction reliably outperforming over the next one to twenty days. Its implementation leaves much of that signal on the table. The long-only version mostly earns the market return (beta 1.08, the same Sharpe ratio as an equal-weight benchmark), and the fully hedged long-short version, comparable to the other two strategies, has a Sharpe ratio of 1.38 before fees but trades about 100 times a year and gives up almost half of its edge to costs. Both weaken in the holdout, and the hedged version's edge has faded since 2013. Measuring the shortfall cumulatively rather than over three days helps most: with the same trading rules, the cumulative residual score raises the long-short Sharpe ratio after fees to 1.76 (1.26 in the holdout) and keeps 1.07 when trades are executed a day late. A daily version of the same signal reaches 3.01, but it collapses to 0.25 with a one-day delay. Most of its profit comes from next-day price reversals that a real trader could probably not capture. Finally, the contrast between in-sample and out-of-sample results shows why rolling estimation matters. Estimating the model on the full sample does not merely inflate performance; for Treynor--Black it reverses the conclusion about which way to trade.
